{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791888607,"nonce":"M8RIHqoAb+K0OlkCplAiP6UNg9Q=","title":"Preserve declared types through generic iteration and abstract field resolvers","message":"The RTTI reader exposes two independent lowering gaps. Lambda.has([\"field\"], \"field\") returns false because for-in replaces a bare element type parameter with Dynamic. Keeping that parameter also requires bit-preserving erasure for Float comparisons and an explicit Dynamic binding for Iterable\u003cDynamic\u003e backed by Array\u003cDynamic\u003e.\n\nA separate @:op(a.b) field read is typed Dynamic before HIR rewrites the operation. Calls such as x.att.a.split(\":\").iterator() lose String and Array types. Fields named get or set are instead synthesized as references to the wrapped Xml methods, even when the field resolver should handle them.\n\nFocused regressions: GenericIterableValues.hx and XmlAccessFieldTypes.hx. Validation pending.","files":null}]}